says she was repeatedly struck in the  legs with an axe when she was detained  in a concentration camp run by british  colonialists  it was in the 1950s her husband was one  of the leaders of a rebel movement  frustrated by colonial land grabbing its  fighters wanted kenya to have  independence from british rule  they never apologized neither did we get  compensation we just came home and  suffered in poverty i spent so much time  in detention and our children suffered  throughout  researchers say british colonial  authorities detained hundreds of  thousands of people both fighters known  as mao mao and suspected sympathisers  and killed tens of thousands of them one  of the camps was right here it's now  part of a school the teachers say  this building was where new prisoners  were held before being taken in here for  interrogations  castration and rape were used to extract  confessions in intelligence as well as  beatings so severe they were often fatal  the crackdown since came to be known as  one of the worst atrocities of the  british empire as the guns sounded a  salute  an empire of which queen elizabeth  became the figurehead when she was  crowned in 1952  she was visiting kenya a british colony  at the time when her father died and she  became queen  the mao mao rebellion and the brutal  crackdown began shortly after  the british government didn't  acknowledge the atrocities for more than  half a century until nine years ago when  it compensated a group of about five  thousand torture victims the one who was  asking  was among the lawyers representing them  in court in london he says the  settlement was a step forward but about  200 000 more victims or their surviving  descendants were left destitute and  deserve compensation for abuses the  passing away of her majesty the queen  presents  a wonderful opportunity for the kenya  government to come out even as they  congratulate the new king  come out  and request the british government to  place their part  in bringing an honorable closure to this  matter  wasn't among the group who were  compensated and says she can't afford a  lawyer  neither is she among those on social  media expressing anger at britain's  monarchy or former empire following the  queen's death  but she says it's still not too late for  the british government to say sorry and  make amends  malcolm webb al jazeera muyga kenya
 

